  "prompt_text": "This figure is a conceptual cross-section of a confined plasma showing how magnetic islands and the surrounding plasma flows interact. A tilted, elongated green structure cuts across the plasma and is labeled with toroidal flow, while blue streamlines on both sides indicate broader poloidal circulation. The image emphasizes that the island modifies local flow patterns and experiences several competing forces and torques.\n\nAround the island, multiple labeled arrows mark resistive and flow-related effects. Near the left side, forces associated with the surrounding current sheet or coupling region appear to act in opposite directions. In the center and right, additional arrows indicate pressure- or rotation-driven terms, with some acting along the island and others opposing it. A downward arrow near the island interior suggests a braking or damping contribution. The toroidal flow is shown as orange helical paths wrapped around the island, contrasting with the blue poloidal streamlines that bend around it and recirculate.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys a force-balance picture: plasma rotation, magnetic coupling, and localized resistive effects jointly determine the motion, rotation, and stability of the magnetic island within the larger plasma.",
